 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "CHALICE WELL. A spring that has never failed, running at about 25,000 gallons a day at the same cool temperature all year. The water runs red from the iron. Legend says the Holy Grail lies beneath it.",
   "x": 29.0,
   "y": 17.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE TOR 158 m. Isle of Avalon in the legends. St Michael's Tower on the top is all that's left of a medieval church. Wear good boots.",
   "x": 29.0,
   "y": 19.0
  },
  {
   "text": "TO THE TOWER. Seven terraces wrap the hill, some say a medieval maze, some say farming. Either way, your calves will find out.",
   "x": 47.0,
   "y": 10.0
  },
  {
   "text": "GLASTONBURY ABBEY. A monastery for the best part of a thousand years, said to be the oldest in England. In 1191 the monks announced they'd found King Arthur's grave. A convenient discovery.",
   "x": 36.0,
   "y": 34.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE GLASTONBURY THORN. Said to grow from Joseph of Arimathea's staff. This one flowers twice a year, in spring and at Christmas. It's a plant with excellent timing.",
   "x": 49.0,
   "y": 37.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE ABBOT'S KITCHEN. Four fireplaces, a lantern roof, and a medieval workplace still standing. The abbot, famously, didn't wash up.",
   "x": 47.0,
   "y": 40.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE FIELD. Glastonbury Festival, held at nearby Worthy Farm since 1970, when a ticket was a pound and included free milk. Today the milk is extra. The mud is still free.",
   "x": 15.0,
   "y": 32.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE STONE CIRCLE. Modern, built for the festival, and still impressive. The real ones are at Avebury and Stonehenge. They won't be mentioned until later.",
   "x": 23.0,
   "y": 38.0
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "Road to Shepton Mallet closed for resurfacing. Again. Sorry for any inconvenience."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 37,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The first Glastonbury Festival was held at Worthy Farm, Pilton, in September 1970. Tickets cost one pound and included free milk.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "glastonbury_first_festival",
   "title": "The First Festival"
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 37,
   "plaque": "glastonbury_dunstan",
   "title": "St Dunstan",
   "text": "Dunstan became abbot here around 940, rebuilt the abbey and later rose to Archbishop of Canterbury. He was a skilled metalworker too, and legend says he once seized the Devil by the nose with red hot tongs. The Devil has given blacksmiths a wide berth since."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 31,
   "plaque": "glastonbury_lake_village",
   "title": "The Lake Village",
   "text": "Over two thousand years ago, Iron Age people built a village of roundhouses on an artificial island in the marsh just outside town. Wet peat kept its timbers and everyday things in fine condition. It was found by Arthur Bulleid, a young local who noticed odd bumps in a field."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 42,
   "plaque": "glastonbury_jerusalem",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "And Did Those Feet",
   "text": "One legend says the young Jesus came to Britain with Joseph of Arimathea. William Blake's poem asks 'And did those feet in ancient time walk upon England's mountains green?' Set to music as the hymn Jerusalem, it is sung everywhere from rugby grounds to village halls."
  },
  {
   "x": 39,
   "y": 19,
   "plaque": "glastonbury_wassail",
   "title": "Wassailing",
   "text": "Each January, Somerset cider makers wassail their orchards: they sing to the apple trees, hang cider soaked toast in the branches for the robins, and make a racket to scare off bad spirits. 'Wassail' comes from the Old English 'wes hal', be in good health."
  }
 ],
